LigParGen can generate the molecule and give the parameters to be used for the simulation. 
The input is the SMILES code of the molecule.

For R123, we have 
https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/9385#section=SMILES
C(C(F)(F)F)(Cl)Cl

For R-134A
https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/1_1_1_2-Tetrafluoroethane
C(C(F)(F)F)F

for R-1234yf
https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/2_3_3_3-Tetrafluoropropene#section=3D-Conformer
C=C(C(F)(F)F)F



If we input this SMILES code into the website 
https://traken.chem.yale.edu/ligpargen/

charge model                         1.14*CM1A-LBCC (Neutral molecules)
Molecule Optimization Iterations     3


we get the files .lmp, .key and .xyz

note that from LigParGen we get some impropers, but the value of K=0. So I ignore them

